 ],
 "literal": "Having left, saying, the day of releasing let come. That, you / if you agreed, please give back and go. / Hill-like heaped sand reaching shore / standing mastwood tree's ground-touching low-lying branch / new heron will roost / O lord of the cool sea shore! You consumed my beauty.",
 "translation": "Let the day come when you say 'I leave' and abandon her. But if you have decided to do so, return her beauty to her before you depart! O lord of the cool seashore, where guest herons roost on the low-hanging branches of the mastwood tree that touch the ground on the shore of hill-like sand dunes, you have consumed her beauty!",
 "modern": "\"நான் உன்னை விட்டுவிடுகிறேன்\" என்று சொல்லி அவளைப் பிரிந்துபோகும் நாள் வரட்டும். ஆனால், நீ அவ்வாறு செய்யத் துணிந்தால், அவளிடம் நீ கவர்ந்த அழகைத் திருப்பித் தந்துவிட்டுப் போவாயாக! குன்றுகளைப் போன்ற மணல் மேடுகள் நிறைந்த கடற்கரையில், நிலத்தைத் தொடும் தாழ்ந்த கிளைகளைக் கொண்ட புன்னை மரத்தில் புதிய நாரைகள் வந்து தங்கும் குளிர்ந்த கடற்கரைத் தலைவனே! நீ அவளது அழகை நுகர்ந்து அழித்துவிட்டாய்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine's friend is speaking to the hero who has been delaying their marriage. She tells him sarcastically that if he wishes to abandon the heroine, he must first return the beauty and health he took from her. Since he cannot return her lost beauty, this is an indirect demand for him to marry her immediately. The description of the coastal landscape (neytal tiṇai) contains an inner meaning (uḷḷuṟai). The guest heron roosting on the low branches of the mastwood tree represents the hero, who came as a temporary guest, enjoyed the heroine's beauty, and might fly away. The key words are 'cērppa' (lord of the shore) and 'nalan' (beauty/virtue).",
